\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{How the selector changes induced interactions}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A comparison of selectors should hold the full interaction fixed.
On the alternating cycle of \ref{fdip-0061}, now use
##{\mathcal V_{\mathrm{tot}}(\phi)=\frac1{24}\sum_{v=0}^3\phi_v^4.}
For opposite retained vertices #{R=\{0,2\}}, add
#{(x_0^4+x_2^4)/24} to that example's first coefficient. The
eliminated covariance is #{s^{-1}I_2}. For adjacent retained vertices
#{R=\{0,1\}}, the eliminated coordinates are #{(\phi_2,\phi_3)},
and
##{\begin{aligned}
D&=\begin{pmatrix}s&-1\\-1&s\end{pmatrix},\\
D^{-1}&=\frac1{s^2-1}\begin{pmatrix}s&1\\1&s\end{pmatrix},\\
\mu(x)&=\frac{t}{s^2-1}
        \begin{pmatrix}1&s\\s&1\end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}x_0\\x_1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{s>1}, so the denominator is positive. These formulas follow
from #{Q_{UR}=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&-t\\-t&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
and the [conditional rules](fdip-0060).}

\p{The adjacent first coefficient is explicitly
##{\begin{aligned}
a_{\mathrm{adj}}(x)={}&\frac{x_0^4+x_1^4}{24}\\
 &+\frac1{24}\sum_{j=2,3}
       \bigl(\mu_j(x)^4+6\mu_j(x)^2v+3v^2\bigr),\\
v={}&\frac{s}{s^2-1}.
\end{aligned}}
In particular its constant term is #{s^2/[4(s^2-1)^2]}, whereas
the opposite selector gives #{1/(4s^2)}. They are unequal for
#{s>1}. The adjacent propagator also has a nonzero cross entry
#{1/(s^2-1)} between eliminated vertices, which contributes pairings
between their quartics in the variance coefficient. Such a cross
entry is absent for opposite elimination. Thus the same precision
and full interaction yield different conditional contraction rules
and induced vertices when the selector changes.}

\p{For a [restricting selector](fdip-005M), #{PQ=QP} makes the
cross block #{K} zero. The Gaussian law then factors between #{X}
and #{Y}, and the eliminated conditional mean is zero. Any remaining
coupling between the two subspaces comes from #{\mathcal V}, not
from a Gaussian cross term. In particular, if
#{\mathcal V(x+y)=\mathcal V_X(x)+\mathcal V_Y(y)}, then
##{F_\lambda(x)=\lambda\mathcal V_X(x)
  -\log\mathbb E_{N(0,D^{-1})}e^{-\lambda\mathcal V_Y}.}
The second term is a formal scalar independent of #{x}. If the
interaction has mixed terms, new retained vertices can still be
induced even though the Gaussian selector restricts.
[Group-algebra selectors](fdip-005N) restrict under the stated
equivariance hypothesis; a subrepresentation selector need not.}

\p{These formulas answer the finite perturbative question: the
propagator and induced interaction depend on the supplied background
and selector. They do not establish convergence of the formal
series, a continuum interacting measure, or a physical mechanism
that selects the background.}
